= Uncertainty — Technical =

== Measurement uncertainty ==
The VIM defines measurement uncertainty as a non-negative parameter characterizing the dispersion of quantity values attributed to a measurand, based on the information used.

A result may be represented through a probability distribution or through summaries such as:
* standard measurement uncertainty;
* combined standard uncertainty;
* expanded uncertainty;
* coverage interval under specified conventions.

Uncertainty propagation can use analytical approximations or Monte Carlo methods depending on the measurement model.

== Epistemic and aleatory decomposition ==
In engineering risk analysis, total predictive uncertainty is often decomposed conceptually into:

<pre>
epistemic uncertainty + aleatory variability
</pre>

Epistemic uncertainty is associated with limited knowledge; aleatory variability with stochastic variation.

The decomposition is model-relative. If a latent causal structure becomes known, what was modeled as random variability may become predictable.

== Bayesian representation ==
Bayesian models represent uncertainty about parameters or hypotheses through probability distributions.

Bayes' rule updates a prior distribution:

<pre>
P(H | E) ∝ P(E | H) P(H)
</pre>

This formalism requires a defined hypothesis/model space and likelihood. Model uncertainty that lies outside the chosen space is not automatically captured.

== Higher-order uncertainty ==
Let <code>Cr(p)</code> denote first-order credence in p.

Higher-order evidence can concern whether the agent's own response <code>Cr(p)</code> is rational or whether the evidence-processing method is reliable.

Reflection principles attempt to constrain first-order credence by higher-order judgments, but their exact formulation is contested.

== Sensitivity and robustness ==
When uncertainty depends on assumptions rather than only random parameters, sensitivity analysis can vary:
* priors;
* model structures;
* parameter ranges;
* missing-data assumptions;
* boundary conditions.

A conclusion is robust when materially relevant conclusions remain stable across a justified set of alternatives.

== Unknown unknowns ==
Formal uncertainty quantification operates within a represented possibility space.

Unknown mechanisms, omitted variables, unmodeled failure modes, or category mistakes may lie outside that space. No probability distribution automatically solves uncertainty about whether the model itself is adequate.

